Janis Papanagnou <janis_papanagnou@hotmail.com> wrote:
> I am wondering what sort of bug classifies it as "Fixed"[1] vs. "fixed"[2]
> at http://nethack.org/v343/bugs.html; I thought it would have to do with
> things like save file compatibility and such. Though, inspecting the bugs
> that seems not necessarily to be the case. What other reasons may there be?

What's certain is that a "fixed" bug should not break save file
compatibility otherwise it couldn't be included in a 3.4.4 release.

> It's also interesting that, compared to "fixed", the percentage of "Fixed"
> bugs increase since around bug #280 (http://random.gridbug.de/fixed.png)
> and I wonder why.

I think on IRC the opinion of the programmers that send bug messages
to the DevTeam - from what could be gathered by the responses of the
DevTeam - is that few or maybe even none DevTeam members have a
working 3.4.4 development version.

If there is no specific bug-fix development branch of 3.4 and
then it is clear that you don't go check if the "Fixed" bug is also a
"fixed" bug.

As a bug-fix release is more unlikely than a major release (it should
be easier than a major release, why hasn't it happened by now?) why do
extra unnecessary work? Developers don't like to do extra work. :)

